# Half-Life satchel detonator. Measured by vrcardgen, finished by hand.
#
# Part J: "The detonator is a radio in the hand (Half-Life ships
# v_satchel_radio.mdl); press its button with the thumb."
#
# The model animates two things in `fire`: `Object02` slides 0.27 units -
# that is the button going down - and `Cylinder03` turns 0.6 radians, which
# is the aerial. The button is the one a thumb wants.
#
# It carries no rounds and no feed rules of any kind. What it needs is a
# control the thumb can find without looking, which is Part G's job, and a
# joint for the button so the press is visible.

weapon v_satchel_radio
match  bones 35  seqs 5  hash 0x272e5180

# The bone every position in this card is measured in - the controls, the
# grab points, the chamber, the ejection port. Nothing recorded it until
# the engine had to place a control in the world to ask whether a thumb is
# near it; the simulator never needed one, because it never works in world
# space. Taken from the generator, which has always called this bone the
# WEAPON BODY and is what measured these numbers in the first place.
body   "Cylinder02"

joint 0  bone "Object02"  slide  travel 0.27  rest 0
    catch sear  at 0.80  holds -1
    grab  point 0.00 0.00 0.00  radius 1.5

# MEASURED, and the kind is now honoured. The button is its own joint -
# Object02, 61 vertices - and this is their centroid in the body frame.
#
# `kind button` used to be silently discarded: the simulator forced every
# control naming a sear to the thumbstick, on the reasoning that a hammer is
# pulled back. A sear is not a hammer, and a detonator is a button you press.
# VRC_HAMMER exists now and only a hammer is forced to the stick.
control sear  at 1.33 0.80 -1.78  finger thumb  kind button

# The satchels themselves are objects in the world - thrown, or set down
# gently where you want them - which is Part I and not a card.
